# 111 DECLARES NOTHING AT ALL, and it is PHASE 85'S KIND -- the code runs, and the
# instrument CANNOT SEE IT.  That is measured here rather than inferred, and it is the
# reason the phase owes probes of its own.  Of the 102 screen cases, 95 ring the bell
# once and 7 not at all, and NOT ONE rings it twice -- so vim_beep's 500 ms rate limit,
# the only clock reading a screen case could reach, is never asked to suppress anything.
# Three controls built from this phase's own source say the same thing from the other
# side: a clock that NEVER ADVANCES, one that RUNS BACKWARDS and one that runs 1000x
# FAST each move 0 of the 102.  (Phase 109's control moved six, and it was not a clock
# control: swapping musl_gettimeofday's two output fields makes each reading an
# independent random number rather than a consistently wrong one.)
#
# WHAT THE PHASE DOES.  The core's whole use of time is STAMP NOW, then ASK HOW MANY
# MILLISECONDS HAVE PASSED, at four places -- do_sleep's `done < msec` loop, vim_beep's
# 500 ms rate limit, handle_osc's `>= p_ost` timeout and inchar_loop's deadline -- and
# not one of them reads a field, prints a reading or compares two stamps.  So phase 109's
# `elapsed_T`, its tagless mirror of `struct timeval`, and `elapsed()`, whose entire body
# is one clock read and one subtraction, and `musl_gettimeofday(long *, long *)`, whose
# out-parameter pair existed only because a struct could not cross the boundary, all go
# together and are replaced by `long musl_now_ms(void)`.  A stamp is `X = musl_now_ms();`
# and a reading is `musl_now_ms() - X`.  80,232 -> 80,222 lines; the binary is 788,488
# bytes on both sides, the same size and not the same bytes.
#
# `nm -u` IS THE SAME 17 NAMES AND `gettimeofday` IS STILL ONE OF THEM, which is worth
# saying because a reader expects a clock phase to free a clock symbol.  It cannot: the
# host still calls `gettimeofday` to implement musl_now_ms, and a symbol leaves when its
# last CALLER leaves the FILE, which is the split and not this phase.
#
# SO THE EVIDENCE IS FOUR FULL RECORDINGS AND FOUR PROBES.  The recordings -- of the
# binary the phase was handed, of its own, and of two variants below -- are byte-identical
# across all 106 records.  The probes are built on `gs`, which is nv_g_cmd's `s` arm and
# is do_sleep(count * 1000): the one call site a keystroke file can drive and the only
# way real time passes inside the editor.  `1gs` and `2gs` take ~1,010 ms and ~2,006 ms
# on both binaries -- do_sleep's loop caps a wait at 1,000 ms, so 2gs is two iterations
# and 1gs is one -- and `hgshh` rings 2 bells on both, which is vim_beep's threshold in
# BOTH directions in one probe: h at column 0 rings, gs lets a second pass, the next h
# rings because more than 500 ms have gone and the third is suppressed because fewer than
# 500 have.  Each half fails on a control aimed at it: with the clock 1000x fast `2gs`
# is 1,006 ms against `1gs` 1,010, and with a clock that never advances `1gs` NEVER
# RETURNS; with vim_beep's 500 written 500000 `hgshh` rings 1 bell and with it written
# -1 it rings 3.  THE BELL CONTROLS MOVE THE THRESHOLD AND NOT THE CLOCK, because a
# clock control that breaks the rate limit breaks do_sleep first and `hgshh` then blocks.
#
# TWO DECISIONS, AND BOTH ARE MEASURED IN THE CHECK.  What musl_now_ms RETURNS is
# milliseconds since the whole second of its first call, not since 1970: every core use
# is a difference, so the origin is free, and on a target where `long` is 32 bits
# `tv_sec * 1000` is signed overflow on the FIRST call for ever -- undefined, and
# reported as such by -fsanitize=signed-integer-overflow -- where `(tv_sec - base) * 1000`
# is exact for 24.86 days of uptime.  The origin being a WHOLE SECOND is what makes it
# behaviourally invisible: musl_now_ms is then epoch-milliseconds less a constant, so a
# DIFFERENCE of two readings is identical under both choices, and the `epoch` variant's
# probes and full recording are the product's.  And the ROUNDING point moves -- elapsed()
# subtracted and THEN divided, musl_now_ms divides at each reading -- which over
# 20,000,000 random pairs is a difference of EXACTLY +-1 ms and never more, 24.95% low,
# 50.08% equal, 24.97% high, with neither formula closer to the truth (+0.4995 ms against
# +0.4996 ms).  No caller can see it: the `ceil` variant rounds every reading UP instead
# of down, which perturbs each one by up to a full millisecond -- twice what the change
# can -- and its probes and its full recording are the product's too.
